<h1>Video 017 — Increment, decrement, and arithmetic operators</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Operators &amp; Assignments Part-1  || Increment &amp; Decrement , Arithmetic</p>
<p>English captions were not available (YouTube only offered Hindi auto-captions of this upload, which do not track the lecture). Video 016 ends Language Fundamentals. Video 018 opens with “last session we covered arithmetic operators and increment and decrement; now string concatenation +.” These notes are that Part-1 operators lecture.</p>
<h2>What this lecture covers</h2>
<ol><li>Operators &amp; Assignments module starts here</li></ol>
<ol><li>Increment and decrement: pre vs post</li></ol>
<ol><li>What ++ may be applied to (and what is a compile error)</li></ol>
<ol><li>The byte b++ vs b = b + 1 difference (the most famous example)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Arithmetic operators and result-type rule (max(int, type of a, type of b))</li></ol>
<ol><li>/ and % with integers vs floating point: ArithmeticException, Infinity, NaN</li></ol>
<ol><li>String + is next lecture — only a teaser if mentioned</li></ol>
<h2>Detailed notes</h2>
<h3>Increment and decrement</h3>
<p>Operators: ++ (plus one) and -- (minus one).</p>
<p>int x = 10;
int y = ++x;  // x = 11, y = 11
int x2 = 10;
int y2 = x2++; // y2 = 10, x2 = 11</p>
<p>int x = 10;
System.out.println(++x); // 11, x is 11
int a = 10;
System.out.println(a++); // 10, a is 11
System.out.println(a);   // 11</p>
<p>Durga’s spoken shortcut: pre means “first change, then remaining operation”; post means “first remaining operation, then change.”</p>
<h3>What you may apply `++` / `--` to</h3>
<p>Must be a variable (something that can be stored back).</p>
<p>int x = 10;
x++;          // valid
++x;          // valid
final int y = 10;
y++;          // CE — cannot assign to final
10++;         // CE — increment needs a variable, not a literal
(x+1)++;      // CE — (x+1) is a value, not a variable</p>
<p>Cannot nest:</p>
<p>int x = 10;
++(++x);      // CE</p>
<p>Cannot apply to `boolean`:</p>
<p>boolean b = true;
b++;          // CE</p>
<p>Cannot apply to a `double`? You can — floating variables accept ++ (x = x + 1 with promotion). Durga’s invalid list is: literals, expressions, final, boolean, nested ++.</p>
<h3>The `byte` puzzle (memorize cold)</h3>
<p>byte b = 10;
b++;          // valid</p>
<p>byte b = 10;
b = b + 1;    // CE: possible loss of precision — int to byte</p>
<p>Why: ++ is internally like b = (type of b) (b + 1). The compiler inserts a cast back to byte.</p>
<p>b = b + 1 uses the arithmetic rule: byte + int → int. Assigning int to byte is CE without a cast.</p>
<p>byte b = 10;
b = (byte) (b + 1);  // valid — this is what ++ does</p>
<p>Same for short and char:</p>
<p>char ch = &#x27;a&#x27;;
ch++;                 // valid — becomes &#x27;b&#x27;
ch = ch + 1;          // CE</p>
<p>short s = 10;
s++;                  // valid
s = s + 1;            // CE</p>
<p>int and wider: both forms are fine because int + int is int.</p>
<p>int x = 10;
x = x + 1;  // valid
x++;        // valid</p>
<p>Overflow: byte b = 127; b++; wraps to -128 (silent two’s complement). No exception. Exam may ask the printed value.</p>
<h3>Expression questions</h3>
<pre class='code'>int x = 10;
int y = x++ + ++x + x--;</pre>
<p>Walk left to right, applying post/pre at each term. Durga spends a long time on these. Method:</p>
<ol><li>Write current x</li></ol>
<ol><li>For each operand, if post: use then change; if pre: change then use</li></ol>
<ol><li>Sum</li></ol>
<p>Example:</p>
<p>int x = 10;
int y = x++;    // y=10, x=11</p>
<p>A typical board sum:</p>
<p>int x = 4;
int y = x++ + ++x;  // 4 + 6 = 10, final x = 6</p>
<p>(After x++, x is 5; ++x makes 6 and uses 6.)</p>
<p>Always recompute; don’t memorize one magic total.</p>
<h3>Arithmetic operators</h3>
<p>Binary: + - * / %</p>
<p>Unary: +x -x</p>
<p>+ on strings is concatenation — next lecture. In this lecture, + is numeric addition unless a String is already in the expression.</p>
<h3>Result type rule</h3>
<p>For a + b (and - * / % on numbers):</p>
<p>Result type = `max(int, type of a, type of b)`</p>
<p>“Max” in the family: byte/short/char → int → long → float → double.</p>
<p>Consequences:</p>
<p>byte a = 10, b = 20;
byte c = a + b;     // CE — a+b is int
int i = a + b;      // valid (30)</p>
<p>byte a = 10;
int b = 20;
int c = a + b;      // valid</p>
<p>int a = 10;
float b = 20.5f;
float c = a + b;    // 30.5f</p>
<p>long a = 10L;
double b = 2.0;
double c = a * b;</p>
<p>char + char is int (Unicode values added):</p>
<p>System.out.println(&#x27;a&#x27; + &#x27;b&#x27;);  // 97+98 = 195</p>
<h3>Division and remainder — integers</h3>
<p>System.out.println(10 / 2);   // 5
System.out.println(10 / 3);   // 3  (truncates toward zero)
System.out.println(10 % 3);   // 1</p>
<p>Integer divide by zero:</p>
<p>System.out.println(10 / 0);   // Runtime: ArithmeticException: / by zero
System.out.println(10 % 0);   // ArithmeticException</p>
<p>ArithmeticException is an unchecked RuntimeException. No throws needed. Program crashes unless you catch it.</p>
<p>There is no integer Infinity.</p>
<h3>Division — floating point</h3>
<p>System.out.println(10.0 / 0);    // Infinity
System.out.println(-10.0 / 0);   // -Infinity
System.out.println(0.0 / 0.0);   // NaN
System.out.println(-0.0 / 0.0);  // NaN</p>
<p>10 / 0.0 — one operand is double (or float), so floating rules apply → Infinity, not exception.</p>
<p>System.out.println(10 / 0.0);    // Infinity
System.out.println(10.0 / 0);    // Infinity</p>
<p>Constants:</p>
<ul><li>Float.POSITIVE_INFINITY / Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY</li></ul>
<ul><li>NEGATIVE_INFINITY</li></ul>
<ul><li>Float.NaN / Double.NaN</li></ul>
<p>NaN is not equal to itself:</p>
<p>System.out.println(Float.NaN == Float.NaN);  // false
System.out.println(Float.isNaN(0.0f / 0.0f)); // true</p>
<h3>`%` with floating point</h3>
<p>Java allows 10.0 % 0.0 → NaN, and 10.5 % 3 → 1.5. This is different from some students’ C mental model.</p>
<h3>Unary minus</h3>
<p>int x = 10;
System.out.println(-x);  // -10</p>
<p>-10 is unary minus on the literal 10, still an int.</p>
<h3>What this lecture is not</h3>
<ul><li>+ with &quot;durga&quot; + 10 → video 018</li></ul>
<ul><li>== vs equals → 018</li></ul>
<ul><li>instanceof, bitwise → 019</li></ul>
<ul><li>&amp;&amp; vs &amp; → 020</li></ul>
<h2>Code from the lecture</h2>
<pre class='code'>class Operators1 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        byte b = 10;
        b++;
        // b = b + 1; // CE

        int x = 10;
        System.out.println(x++); // 10
        System.out.println(++x); // 12

        System.out.println(&#x27;a&#x27; + 1);     // 98
        System.out.println(10 / 0.0);    // Infinity
        System.out.println(0.0 / 0.0);   // NaN
        try {
            System.out.println(10 / 0);
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println(&quot;int / 0 → &quot; + e);
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h2>Rules to memorize</h2>
<ol><li>++/-- need a non-final variable; no literals, no nested ++, no boolean.</li></ol>
<ol><li>b++ is valid for byte/short/char; b = b + 1 is CE.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Arithmetic result type = max(int, typeof a, typeof b).</li></ol>
<ol><li>byte + byte is int.</li></ol>
<ol><li>int / 0 → ArithmeticException; floating / 0 → Infinity / -Infinity / NaN.</li></ol>
<ol><li>NaN == NaN is false.</li></ol>
<h2>Exam / interview traps</h2>
<ul><li>byte b = 10; b = b + 1; CE vs b++ valid.</li></ul>
<ul><li>127 as byte then ++ → -128.</li></ul>
<ul><li>10 / 0 vs 10.0 / 0 — exception vs Infinity.</li></ul>
<ul><li>&#x27;a&#x27; + &#x27;b&#x27; prints 195, not ab (no String involved).</li></ul>
<ul><li>final int x = 10; x++; CE.</li></ul>
<h2>Recap</h2>
<p>Operators module is open. Next: + as concatenation, then relational and equality.</p>
<p>Next video: 018-concatenation-relational-equality.md</p>
